  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>

<div class="os-raise-familysupport">
  <para>Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b62c9150250bf52d6cc37569213ab516e0090dca">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>
</div>

<para>In an exponential function, the output is multiplied by the same factor every time the input increases by one. The multiplier is called the growth factor.</para>
<para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Refer back to your work in the table in activity 5.4.3: Exponential Change: The Growth Factor, where \(n=500 \cdot 2^t\) and \(p=100 \cdot 3^t\). Use that information and the given coordinate planes to graph the following using the Desmos tool below. </para>
<para>Use the equation \(n=500 \cdot 2^t\) to answer questions 1 – 7</para>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0" data-bottom="0" data-top="9000" data-left="0" data-right="5"/>


<!--Q#1-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the domain? </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>The graph of the function shows that all real values of t define the domain. \((-\infty, +\infty)\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->



<!--Q#2-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the range? </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>The graph of the function shows that all real values of n greater than 0 define the range. \((0, +\infty)\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#3-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> The scenario dictates that the domain and range be restricted in comparison to the graph of the function that models it. Write a reasonable domain and range based on the scenario using inequalities. </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>Domain: \(0 \leq t &lt; +\infty\); because we begin measuring time at 0 hours.</para>

  

  <para>Range: \(500 \leq n \lt +\infty\); because we begin with 500 bacteria in the lab experiment.</para>



</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#4-->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> Graph \((t,n)\) when \(t\) is 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. </item>
</list>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0" data-bottom="0" data-top="9000" data-left="0" data-right="5"/>

<example><exercise>

  <problem>

  
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para><figure><media alt="A discrete graph."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/37a00581b3abd08d729d2ba1239f30105cc37689"/></media></figure></para>

</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#5-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> On the graph of \(n\), where can you see each number that appears in the equation? </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>500 is the vertical intercept (or the value of \(n\) when \(t\) is 0). The 2 is the doubling of the \(n\) value every time \(t\) increases by 1.</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> Graph \((t,p)\) when \(t\) is 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. (If you get stuck, you can create a table.) </item>
</list>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0" data-bottom="0" data-top="9000" data-left="0" data-right="5"/>
<!--Q#6-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
   
  
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para><figure><media alt="A discrete graph."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7fba9b9732a9cb1c08657bac7e1b245f83eb1f88"/></media></figure></para>

</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#7-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> On the graph of \(p\), where can you see each number that appears in the equation? </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>100 is the vertical intercept (or the value of \(p\) when \(t\) is 0). The 3 is the tripling of the \(p\) value every time \(t\) increases by 1</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->


